📖 WHAT IS AN ALIAS?
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

An alias is a temporary name given to a column or table within a SQL query. Aliases
improve readability and can make expressions easier to reference. Aliases only exist
for the duration of the query.

TWO MAIN TYPES OF ALIASES:
• Column aliases: rename query output columns
• Table aliases: shorten table references and clarify joins

USAGE:
• In SELECT lists to rename columns
• In JOINs to shorten table references
• In expressions and functions to label results
• In subqueries as temporary table names


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
🔤 ALIAS SYNTAX
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Column alias syntax:
SELECT column_name AS alias_name
FROM table_name;

Table alias syntax:
SELECT column_name(s)
FROM table_name AS alias_name;

Note: AS is optional in most cases.
SELECT column_name alias_name FROM table_name;
SELECT table_name alias_name


