⚠️ WHEN TO USE ALIASES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Aliases are useful when:
• Multiple tables are involved in a query
• Functions or expressions produce new columns
• Column names are long or unclear
• Two or more columns use the same name
• You want clean output headers for reporting


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
⚙️ BEST PRACTICES
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. Use AS for clarity:
   SELECT column_name AS alias_name
2. Use quotes for aliases with spaces:
   SELECT column_name AS "Alias Name"
3. Keep aliases short but meaningful:
   SELECT CustomerName AS Name
4. Use table aliases in multi-table joins:
   FROM Customers AS c JOIN Orders AS o ON c.CustomerID=o.CustomerID
5. Avoid alias names that duplicate reserved words or column names.
6. Refer to aliases only in the outer SELECT list, not in the same SELECT clause expression.


